      "url": "https://standardkernel.com/blog/intel-gaudi2-outperforms-h100/",
      "date": "2026-11-27",
      "authors": [
        "anneouyang"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how systems-level software tuning can bridge severe software ecosystem gaps and unlock superior inference performance on alternative hardware.",
      "hn_id": "50665420",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50665420",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Silicon specifications do not dictate actual inference throughput; runtime compilers and custom compute kernels do. Recent production benchmarks showed that custom software optimization alone increased Intel Gaudi2 cached-prefill throughput by 3.21x and decode throughput by 2.84x while serving Qwen3.5-27B.\n\nHardware teams frequently blame poor silicon adoption on physical architecture limitations, but the actual bottleneck remains ecosystem software maturity. With custom kernels, Gaudi2 achieved 60 percent higher decode throughput than an NVIDIA H100 80GB PCIe card at roughly 17 percent of the hourly rental price, yielding a 9.4x improvement in output tokens per dollar.\n\nModern large language model deployments cannot afford to treat underlying accelerator runtimes as static black boxes. Extracting peak performance requires profiling memory bandwidth saturation, prefill scheduling, and cache layouts directly at the compiler layer rather than waiting on upstream framework patches.\n\nHardware diversity will only succeed in production if software engineers master low-level kernel optimization."
    },
    {
