      "content": "Autonomous agents are shifting from speculative coding assistants to reliable execution engines on complex, multi-step tasks. AWS evaluated their penetration testing agent across 37 recent, real-world CVE targets spanning web frameworks, ML platforms, and ecommerce suites. The agent was provided only an endpoint URL and a single-line hint, operating from an unauthenticated or low-privilege boundary.\n\nThe system achieved a flag capture rate of 89.2 percent (33 out of 37) by discovering the vulnerability, chaining exploit primitives, and extracting secrets without human intervention. Two additional targets were breached via alternate unintended paths, while two failures occurred because the exploit inadvertently knocked the services offline before retrieving the verification token.\n\nThis benchmark demonstrates genuine agentic reasoning: synthesizing exploit payloads, adjusting to environment responses, and chaining security primitives across eight vulnerability classes including SQL injection and SSRF. For platform engineers, this level of autonomous execution marks an inflection point in how teams must design automated vulnerability testing and hardened sandboxes."
    },
    {
      "title": "Preserving timeseries properties at arbitrary scales using statistical moments",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://nathanormond.substack.com/p/statistical-moments-the-history-of",
      "date": "2026-11-27",
      "authors": [
        "Nathan Ormond"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how to use statistical moments to downsample timeseries data without sacrificing analytical fidelity at arbitrary scales. It provides a durable framework for designing storage-efficient data ingestion pipelines that preserve essential statistical properties.",
      "hn_id": "50670185",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50670185",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Naive time-series downsampling usually relies on simple windowed averages, minimums, or maximums. While this shrinks storage footprints rapidly, it destroys higher-order statistical properties. Once you roll up fine-grained data into coarse intervals, reconstructing true variance, skewness, or kurtosis across arbitrary query windows becomes mathematically impossible.\n\nStoring statistical moments directly within your ingestion rollups changes this equation. By recording the count, sum, sum of squares, and higher raw moments in your persistence layer, you can combine arbitrary intervals without re-querying the underlying raw data. This preserves distribution shapes across different time scales while retaining the storage advantages of downsampling.\n\nApplying this pattern allows analytical queries to compute accurate standard deviations and anomaly thresholds over months of historical records at aggregate speed. When designing telemetry pipelines, do not just discard points to save bytes; preserve the underlying distribution."
    },
    {
