      "content": "Local testing for cloud infrastructure has long suffered from painful trade-offs. Engineers either settle for slow, shared staging environments that trigger permission bottlenecks, or rely on lightweight mocks that fail to replicate authentic IAM and API behavior.\n\nLocally addresses this friction for Azure by emulating 39 Resource Providers and 18 data plane engines directly on your machine. It provides genuine role-based access control, managed identities, and policy enforcement offline. Because state runs locally, you can snapshot environments or deliberately inject chaos delays across ARM and data planes to test failure modes deterministically.\n\nIntegrating directly with tools like Terraform and the Azure CLI with zero configuration overrides keeps feedback loops tight. Offline cloud emulation eliminates staging contention while catching configuration errors before they hit your real infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "title": "Large batching makes massive models viable on consumer hardware",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://alexselzer.com/posts/large-batch-local-inference/",
      "date": "2026-11-27",
      "authors": [
        "Alexander Selzer"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how massive batch sizes amortize offloaded expert weight transfers to achieve fast local inference on consumer GPUs.",
      "hn_id": "50668282",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50668282",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Running large Mixture-of-Experts models on consumer hardware typically forces severe quantization compromises, like dropping precision to 2 bits or skipping unallocated experts. A detailed benchmark running DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash across four 16 GB AMD Radeon GPUs reveals a far better trade-off: scaling the batch size instead of mutilating model weights.\n\nWith a massive 416 GB model footprint hosted across 64 GB of VRAM, single-request inference crawled at 25 to 35 tokens per second because offloaded experts were transferred over the bus for just a couple of tokens. However, because the compressed KV cache consumed only 10 MB per GPU per thousand tokens, the hardware could comfortably process hundreds of concurrent queries.\n\nScaling to a batch size of 500 requests amortized the transfer overhead of the MXFP4 experts across all concurrent sequences. Aggregate generation surged to 228 tokens per second on text-to-SQL workloads, slashing energy consumption from 12.8 to 1.29 watt-hours per thousand tokens.\n\nWhen memory bandwidth becomes your operational bottleneck, maximizing batch concurrency turns sparse memory transfers into sustained compute efficiency."
    },
    {
      "title": "SMS pumping inflates telecom bills while dashboards stay healthy",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://signalgate.ai/blog/sms-pumping-attack",
      "date": "2026-11-27",
      "authors": [
        "vasiliyasp"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how SMS pumping attacks bypass standard telemetry and rate limits by weaponizing unverified verification requests. You will learn the mechanics behind artificially inflated traffic and how to detect it before receiving costly carrier bills.",
      "hn_id": "50667080",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50667080",
      "image": null,
      "content": "SMS pumping attacks expose a subtle blind spot in standard production monitoring: every system metric reports green while your billing costs skyrocket.\n\nIn typical artificially inflated traffic schemes, bot networks request one-time passwords across distributed IP blocks without ever attempting to verify the codes. Because requests succeed with HTTP 200 responses, error rate alarms never fire, latency graphs stay flat, and traditional CAPTCHAs or per-IP rate limits fall flat against distributed mobile networks.\n\nThe critical telemetry gap is the conversion funnel between issued codes and verified tokens. When you track verification conversion rather than raw traffic volume, this fraud becomes immediately visible.\n\nProtecting authentication endpoints requires inspecting telecom carrier metadata and funnel conversion before triggering billable provider APIs."
    },
    {
