      "content": "Running autonomous coding agents with shell access introduces significant containment risks that software permissions cannot solve.\n\nWhen you pair an LLM agent with fuzzing harnesses, the model must trigger crashes and execute generated test inputs. Relying on API permission prompts quickly fails when you want unattended test loops that discover defects and write fixes on the fly.\n\nThe Prospero harness solves this problem by executing sessions entirely inside unnetworked Docker or Podman containers with mounted Git repositories. The agent has full internal execution rights to compile code, run instrumented binaries, and commit local patches, but zero network access to exfiltrate data or touch remote remotes.\n\nTrue agent containment belongs at the operating system isolation layer, not inside LLM client prompt guards."
    },
    {
      "title": "Migrating Agent Swarm from SQLite to PostgreSQL in six days",
