      "date": "2026-11-27",
      "authors": [
        "Dani",
        "Taras"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn the concrete constraints and performance bottlenecks that force a transition from embedded SQLite to PostgreSQL at scale. It offers practical insights on executing a fast, test-driven database switch without introducing an ORM.",
      "hn_id": "50665911",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50665911",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Starting with SQLite is often the right call for early-stage systems, but embedded engines eventually hit a ceiling under high-concurrency workloads. When Agent Swarm scaled their single SQLite database to 87 GB, individual long-running statements began stalling the entire Bun event loop because available in-process drivers execute synchronously.\n\nRather than refactoring with an ORM, the team enforced dual-database compatibility by keeping raw SQL behind a thin storage interface. They established a strict testing rule: all end-to-end integration tests had to pass simultaneously on both SQLite and PostgreSQL without branching business logic.\n\nThis approach proves that swapping persistence tiers under live multi-agent loads does not require months of over-engineering. Choosing minimal abstraction boundaries early allows you to migrate from embedded files to distributed databases in days.\n\nClean boundaries beat heavy abstractions every time."
    },
    {
      "title": "PostgreSQL views fail to deliver clean abstractions in production",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://boringsql.com/posts/strong-views/",
      "date": "2026-11-27",
      "authors": [
        "Tomte"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the hidden operational pitfalls of PostgreSQL views and how layering them compromises database maintainability.",
      "hn_id": "50666254",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50666254",
      "image": null,
      "content": "PostgreSQL views look like clean abstractions on paper, but they frequently backfire in production schemas. Behind the scenes, standard views are not virtual tables; they are rewrite rules integrated directly into the query planner.\n\nThe problems begin when you layer views on top of other views. When you nest views three or four layers deep, you hide query complexity from your team. A seemingly benign SELECT on a summary view can force the query planner to expand massive subqueries, stripping away optimal index usage and blowing up join paths.\n\nSchema evolution presents an even worse trap. When you define a view with SELECT *, Postgres freezes the column list at definition time. Adding new columns to underlying tables will not propagate to the view, while altering or dropping existing columns triggers cascading DDL locks or migration failures.\n\nKeep your logical layers flat, avoid deep view hierarchies, and treat database views as strict query interfaces rather than architectural building blocks."
    },
    {
      "title": "Proving silent CPU corruption without capturing the machine",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://prince776.dev/blog/proving-mercurial-cores-offline/",
      "date": "2026-11-27",
      "authors": [
        "Prince Gupta"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how elusive hardware defects cause silent data corruption during cryptographic operations and how to verify faulty CPU cores offline without physical access to the node.",
      "hn_id": "50664517",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50664517",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Silent data corruption is one of the most terrifying classes of failures in large-scale storage engines. A single cloud virtual machine can execute an instruction like PCLMULQDQ inside GHASH, hit an undocumented hardware bug, and silently produce a ghost carry bit.\n\nThe real nightmare occurs long after the write completes. The underlying ciphertext is completely intact, but the authentication tag is corrupted, leaving engineers with failed verification requests days or months later. By the time the incident is triaged, cloud orchestrators have usually recycled the suspect node, destroying the offending hardware before anyone can capture it.\n\nAttributing this corruption offline requires deterministic forensic reconstruction. By analyzing the Galois field multiplication steps inside the GHASH pipeline, one can trace the exact arithmetic stage where the flipped bit entered the intermediate computation.\n\nTreating cloud hardware as fundamentally non-deterministic is a sobering operational requirement. Defensive architecture must focus on mathematical proof of hardware failure rather than expecting cloud instances to stay frozen for debugging."
    },
    {
      "title": "Deterministic lockfiles secure AI coding agents against remote execution",
