      "content": "Quantization techniques like AWQ, Hadamard rotations, and GPTQ interact in unpredictable ways when stacked together. Finding the optimal recipe for a given model architecture and target precision usually requires weeks of manual experimentation.\n\nMIT HAN Lab introduced QDA, a closed-loop coding agent that autonomously searches quantization recipes without human intervention. Instead of relying on slow evaluations that consume dozens of GPU-hours, the agent utilizes a rapid KL divergence metric to evaluate candidate pipelines in minutes.\n\nIn testing on GLM-5.3-Flash at NVFP4, an automated 6.5-hour search costing 69 dollars recovered 98.3 percent of original FP8 accuracy. Using LLM agents as feedback-driven optimizers unlocks practical hardware efficiency gains that static recipes consistently miss."
    },
    {
