      "why_read": "Read this to understand how calibrated survival curves predict LLM session idle times to optimize KV cache tiering across GPU, CPU, and disk.",
      "hn_id": "50664399",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50664399",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Managing key-value cache memory across idle LLM sessions is one of the most expensive operational headaches in modern inference serving. If you keep the cache in GPU memory, you waste scarce high-bandwidth memory on idle sessions. If you evict it too soon, the subsequent turn forces an expensive full prefill computation.\n\nLayby-Dwell addresses this trade-off by predicting the exact idle duration of a session using an auxiliary thirty-four million parameter two-tower model. Instead of relying on arbitrary timeout heuristics or LRU caches, it generates a calibrated survival curve across fifteen horizons ranging from half a second to thirty minutes.\n\nRunning entirely on host CPU with zero GPU memory overhead, the model produces placement decisions in roughly ten to twenty milliseconds. The inference engine then balances measured transfer overhead against recompute cost to decide whether to hold the cache in VRAM, offload it to system memory, write it to local NVMe, or drop it entirely.\n\nDynamic survival prediction turns ad-hoc cache eviction into a principled economic decision."
