      "why_read": "Understand how PostgreSQL computes distinct value estimates and why sparse nullable columns cause planner miscalculations. You will learn the statistical mechanics behind these errors and how sampling affects query plans.",
      "hn_id": "50663260",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50663260",
      "image": null,
      "content": "PostgreSQL autovacuum can quietly ruin your query execution plans when a column is mostly null. When a 172 GB table had a column that was null in 99.8 percent of rows, the planner estimate for distinct values swung wildly by more than a factor of three between daily runs, even drifting into negative numbers.\n\nThe culprit lies directly inside analyze.c and the birthday problem. PostgreSQL gathers sample pages across the table to calculate statistics. When a column is almost entirely null, the sample collects very few non-null values. Because the sample size is tiny relative to the actual population of non-null entries, small statistical fluctuations cause the planner to drastically miscalculate distinct values.\n\nWhen distinct count estimates collapse or turn negative, the planner assumes an incorrect selectivity. This leads directly to disastrous plan choices, such as switching from a fast index scan to a punishing sequential scan or an inappropriate nested loop.\n\nYou can prevent these planner swings by either bumping the statistics target to force a larger sample or creating a partial index with an explicit expression that excludes null values entirely.\n\nNever let sparse columns quietly dictate your query planner choices."
